From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Grische <github@grische.xyz>
Date: Fri, 11 Sep 2026 09:28:34 +0200
Subject: net: lantiq: xrx500: drop the no-op ndo_change_mtu, clamp max_mtu

intel_xrx500_ndo_change_mtu() range-checked new_mtu and returned 0
without ever storing it, so netif_set_mtu_ext() ran its whole sequence
-- the NETDEV_PRECHANGEMTU and NETDEV_CHANGEMTU notifiers included --
around a dev->mtu that never moved, and reported success. Every MTU
change through rtnetlink was silently discarded.

The driver has nothing to program on an MTU change: it writes no frame
length register and resizes no ring. Its own comment says the range
check is redundant because the netdev core already enforces min_mtu
and max_mtu, and the function does nothing else. So remove the callback
rather than adding the store. __netif_set_mtu() does that WRITE_ONCE
itself when ndo_change_mtu is NULL, and both kinds of caller are
covered: netif_set_mtu_ext() runs dev_validate_mtu() against
min_mtu/max_mtu before it, and team and bonding call it directly only to
put back an MTU the port already carried. No caller in the tree tests
ndo_change_mtu for presence as a capability.

The bound the driver advertises is wrong as well. netdev->max_mtu is
9216, the size of the CBM jumbo pool's segments, but no frame that large
can leave a port:

  - The switch core these ports sit behind is initialised to discard
    anything longer than a standard Ethernet frame. gsw_flow_pce.c
    writes MAC_FLEN = 1518 on every core but the WAN-side one, with the
    vendor's own comment saying that is meant to drop jumbo frames, and
    the per-port long-frame enables are left clear.

  - intel_xrx500_ndo_start_xmit() checks out a standard 2 KiB FSQM
    segment and drops anything that would not fit inside it after the
    128-byte CBM offset, NET_SKB_PAD and the PMAC header. Nothing in
    the transmit path ever asks for a jumbo segment.

So the two halves have to be fixed in one change. Making the MTU stick
without lowering the ceiling would replace an operation that visibly
does nothing with one that quietly breaks the link: the larger MTU
sticks, the stack starts handing the switch frames it discards, and
because the discard happens past the MAC neither end counts it --
tx_packets advances, tx_dropped and the peer's rx_length_errors do not.

Lower max_mtu to ETH_DATA_LEN, which is the MTU every port already runs
at and the largest the switch is configured to carry; the core then
refuses anything above it with -EINVAL and an extack. That is also the
value ether_setup() installs, so what the driver had really done was
raise the ethernet default; keep the assignment and the comment anyway,
because the reason 1500 is the ceiling on this hardware is not obvious
from the default. Raising it later is not a change to this file: it
needs the switch's frame-length gate opened and the transmit path moved
off the standard segment, in that order.

Assisted-by: Claude:claude-opus-5
Signed-off-by: Grische <github@grische.xyz>

diff --git a/drivers/net/ethernet/lantiq/intel_xrx500.c b/drivers/net/ethernet/lantiq/intel_xrx500.c
index 060aa4245230..fddd52d5e599 100644
--- a/drivers/net/ethernet/lantiq/intel_xrx500.c
+++ b/drivers/net/ethernet/lantiq/intel_xrx500.c
@@ -1018,25 +1018,6 @@ void intel_xrx500_rx_drop_account(struct net_device *dev)
 	atomic_long_inc(&port->stats.rx_dropped);
 }
 
-/**
- * intel_xrx500_ndo_change_mtu() - MTU change handler.
- *
- * @dev:     the source netdev.
- *
- * @new_mtu: the requested new MTU value.
- */
-static int intel_xrx500_ndo_change_mtu(struct net_device *dev, int new_mtu)
-{
-	/*
-	 * Range check is redundant (netdev core already enforced via
-	 * dev->min_mtu / dev->max_mtu) but kept as a defensive belt-and-
-	 * braces guard so an out-of-band caller cannot bypass it.
-	 */
-	if (new_mtu < ETH_MIN_MTU || new_mtu > 9216)
-		return -EINVAL;
-	return 0;
-}
-
 /**
  * intel_xrx500_ndo_get_stats64() - ndo_get_stats64 implementation.
  *
@@ -1063,7 +1044,6 @@ static const struct net_device_ops intel_xrx500_netdev_ops = {
 	.ndo_stop        = intel_xrx500_ndo_stop,
 	.ndo_start_xmit  = intel_xrx500_ndo_start_xmit,
 	.ndo_get_stats64 = intel_xrx500_ndo_get_stats64,
-	.ndo_change_mtu  = intel_xrx500_ndo_change_mtu,
 	.ndo_validate_addr = eth_validate_addr,
 	.ndo_set_mac_address = eth_mac_addr,
 };
@@ -1323,7 +1303,14 @@ static int intel_xrx500_port_setup(struct intel_xrx500_priv *priv,
 	netdev->ethtool_ops = &intel_xrx500_ethtool_ops;
 
 	netdev->min_mtu = ETH_MIN_MTU;
-	netdev->max_mtu = 9216;
+	/*
+	 * The switch core these ports sit behind is initialised to discard
+	 * anything longer than a standard Ethernet frame (gsw_flow_pce.c
+	 * writes a frame length of 1518) and intel_xrx500_ndo_start_xmit()
+	 * only ever checks out a standard 2 KiB CBM segment, so a standard
+	 * MTU is the most a port can carry.
+	 */
+	netdev->max_mtu = ETH_DATA_LEN;
 
 	phylink = phylink_create(&port->phylink_config,
 				 of_fwnode_handle(port_node),
